WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.568 --> 00:00:03.436
-Our next guest is a talented
actress you know from iconic

00:00:03.436 --> 00:00:06.139
films like "Dirty Dancing"
and "Ferris Bueller's Day Off."

00:00:06.139 --> 00:00:09.609
Her memoir, "Out of the Corner,"
is available now.

00:00:09.609 --> 00:00:12.312
Please welcome to the show
Jennifer Grey, everybody!

00:00:12.312 --> 00:00:14.046
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:14.046 --> 00:00:22.221 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:22.221 --> 00:00:30.396 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:30.396 --> 00:00:38.604 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:38.604 --> 00:00:46.779 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:46.779 --> 00:00:51.817
I am far too intimidated to try
to dance with you, Jennifer.

00:00:51.817 --> 00:00:53.787
-You Know, baby.
-Yes?

00:00:53.787 --> 00:00:56.222
-It's all about just connection.

00:00:56.222 --> 00:00:57.490
-Okay.

00:00:57.490 --> 00:01:00.526
-And you just feel it,
and then it just does you.

00:01:00.526 --> 00:01:02.327
-Okay.
-Because I didn't plan that.

00:01:02.327 --> 00:01:04.764
-No, it just came to you.

00:01:04.764 --> 00:01:06.799
-Just bubbled. Just bubbled out.

00:01:06.799 --> 00:01:09.068
-So, dancing has always --
I mean, I imagine you have

00:01:09.068 --> 00:01:11.904
a very loving relationship
with dance.

00:01:11.904 --> 00:01:15.008
-I have a very interesting
relationship with dance.

00:01:15.008 --> 00:01:17.110
I don't know,
do any of you guys dance?

00:01:17.110 --> 00:01:19.245
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:19.245 --> 00:01:21.347
-It's the same people
who never got COVID.

00:01:21.347 --> 00:01:23.549
[ Laughter ]
Crossover, yeah.

00:01:23.549 --> 00:01:25.017
-Well, the thing is,

00:01:25.017 --> 00:01:28.688
is if you love to dance,
you have to dance.

00:01:28.688 --> 00:01:30.890
-And if you're not
a good dancer,

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:32.225
it doesn't [bleep] matter.

00:01:32.225 --> 00:01:33.525
-Okay, interesting.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:33.525 --> 00:01:35.761
I want to ask about this.
So, you write a memoir,

00:01:35.761 --> 00:01:38.598
and obviously a lot of elements
of your life.

00:01:38.598 --> 00:01:40.166
You went back to old diaries.

00:01:40.166 --> 00:01:42.635
Was this something -- Were you
a diarist your whole life?

00:01:42.635 --> 00:01:45.305
-It just sounds disgusting,
"diarist."

00:01:45.305 --> 00:01:46.606
-I know, I'm sorry.
-Blah.

00:01:46.606 --> 00:01:49.175
[ Laughter ]

00:01:49.175 --> 00:01:51.177
-A journalist?
That's worse, yeah.

00:01:51.177 --> 00:01:52.645
-Okay, I don't know.

00:01:52.645 --> 00:01:56.549
All I know is that I wrote from
when I was 14 till I was 41.

00:01:56.549 --> 00:01:58.718
And I wrote, you know,
for myself.

00:01:58.718 --> 00:02:00.886
I wrote just to dump out
whatever I was feeling

00:02:00.886 --> 00:02:02.822
because we didn't have
cellphones.

00:02:02.822 --> 00:02:04.890
We didn't have --
couldn't call anyone.

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:06.526
You couldn't even,
like, pull up a movie.

00:02:06.526 --> 00:02:08.961
It was just, like, you.

00:02:08.961 --> 00:02:12.298
And so then I just felt like I
needed to talk to someone.

00:02:12.298 --> 00:02:14.434
-You're talking like
you grew up in 1920.

00:02:14.434 --> 00:02:16.001
[ Laughter ]
-I'm telling you,

00:02:16.001 --> 00:02:17.570
when I was
in the horse and buggy,

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:19.672
clippity-clop, very hard to --

00:02:19.672 --> 00:02:23.242
No, I mean, literally,
we didn't have the option

00:02:23.242 --> 00:02:25.278
to call someone to say,
"I need to talk."

00:02:25.278 --> 00:02:28.414
So I would just download
how I was feeling,

00:02:28.414 --> 00:02:33.486
and it was usually about a boy
or feeling just so excited

00:02:33.486 --> 00:02:38.858
about a crush or devastated
about having things gone wrong.

00:02:38.858 --> 00:02:42.595
You know, I wasn't writing about
world peace or, you know,

00:02:42.595 --> 00:02:47.633
curing cancer, but I was very
consumed with my emotional life

00:02:47.633 --> 00:02:49.401
and I felt very alone.

00:02:49.401 --> 00:02:52.972
But then I had this listener
that was just very patient.

00:02:52.972 --> 00:02:57.176
And I just wrote,
and I wrote from 14 to 41.

00:02:57.176 --> 00:03:00.546
And it's weird
because I never read them.

00:03:00.546 --> 00:03:02.748
-Oh, wow.
-Until I started the memoir.

00:03:02.748 --> 00:03:05.284
I had them in this box and they
just traveled with me

00:03:05.284 --> 00:03:08.053
from whatever location,
like New York, L.A.,

00:03:08.053 --> 00:03:09.955
wherever I was living,
this trunk.

00:03:09.955 --> 00:03:12.625
And I pulled them up,
and they just smelled musty,

00:03:12.625 --> 00:03:15.194
as you can imagine, like, "ugh."

00:03:15.194 --> 00:03:20.933
And then I'm seeing, and it's
the same person from 14 to 41.

00:03:20.933 --> 00:03:23.136
Like, I thought
I would have matured.

00:03:23.136 --> 00:03:24.270
[ Laughter ]

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:26.506
I thought I would have
learned something.

00:03:26.506 --> 00:03:29.575
But it's the same thing because
it's like the essence,

00:03:29.575 --> 00:03:33.646
like the really inner voice that
is unedited,

00:03:33.646 --> 00:03:35.348
and it's just the same.

00:03:35.348 --> 00:03:38.017
And it's just,
the only difference is it got

00:03:38.017 --> 00:03:40.386
a little more dramatic
and the, you know,

00:03:40.386 --> 00:03:42.221
handwriting got really sloppy.

00:03:42.221 --> 00:03:43.723
[ Laughter ]
Like, towards the end.

00:03:43.723 --> 00:03:45.657
-Yeah, that's the first thing
to go, the handwriting.

00:03:45.657 --> 00:03:49.662
-The first page is always like,
"Oh, this is the new me.

00:03:49.662 --> 00:03:52.532
I am going to be writing with
this perfect calligraphy."

00:03:52.532 --> 00:03:54.000
And at the end, you're just
like, "Grrrr!"

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:55.234
[ Laughter ]

00:03:55.234 --> 00:03:57.036
-So, "Out of the Corner"
obviously references

00:03:57.036 --> 00:03:59.238
a very famous line
in a very famous movie.

00:03:59.238 --> 00:04:01.040
Was there any sense
that you were

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:02.909
taking the line back
for yourself?

00:04:02.909 --> 00:04:05.011
Because, you know, it's belonged
to everybody else

00:04:05.011 --> 00:04:06.946
ever since "Dirty Dancing"
came out.

00:04:06.946 --> 00:04:10.984
-Well, it was -- I noticed there
was an irony about the fact that

00:04:10.984 --> 00:04:13.018
if I died, it would be like,

00:04:13.018 --> 00:04:15.288
"Okay, she's finally
out of the corner."

00:04:15.288 --> 00:04:17.123
Or, like, I don't know what they
would say, but it would --

00:04:17.123 --> 00:04:18.658
-What newspaper?

00:04:18.658 --> 00:04:20.059
[ Laughter ]

00:04:20.059 --> 00:04:22.528
"Jennifer Grey, out of corner
in a coffin."

00:04:22.528 --> 00:04:24.030
[ Laughter ]

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:26.665
-Yes, "she's out of the corner,
good."

00:04:26.665 --> 00:04:29.435
You know, there's just
something -- I just, you know,

00:04:29.435 --> 00:04:32.304
I've noticed that there's
a little mean-spiritedness

00:04:32.304 --> 00:04:35.741
in the world, and everyone's
looking for just a flashy thing.

00:04:35.741 --> 00:04:39.412
And I thought, "What if I'm
known to be the girl

00:04:39.412 --> 00:04:42.782
that nobody puts me
in the corner?"

00:04:42.782 --> 00:04:47.719
And as I was writing my stories,
high points, low points,

00:04:47.719 --> 00:04:51.690
kind of the things that made me
who I am, really,

00:04:51.690 --> 00:04:54.326
those things shape you,
especially the hard things,

00:04:54.326 --> 00:04:56.496
especially the really hard
things.

00:04:56.496 --> 00:04:58.998
They just do something.
They change you.

00:04:58.998 --> 00:05:01.934
And then I thought, "Wait a
minute, there's a lot of stories

00:05:01.934 --> 00:05:04.070
where I'm feeling
put in a corner."

00:05:04.070 --> 00:05:06.905
And then I thought,
"That's so gross."

00:05:06.905 --> 00:05:09.342
Like, "I don't want to be
that girl."

00:05:09.342 --> 00:05:12.111
And I thought,
"Well, how did I get there?"

00:05:12.111 --> 00:05:16.315
And then, once I took away
all blame and all shame

00:05:16.315 --> 00:05:20.186
and I just tried to be as honest
as if I was a detective

00:05:20.186 --> 00:05:24.257
or a scientist, you know, just
laying out a forensics table,

00:05:24.257 --> 00:05:27.793
just to get really cheerful...
[ Laughter ]

00:05:27.793 --> 00:05:32.999
...and what I noticed was that,
yes, things happen to everybody.

00:05:32.999 --> 00:05:35.367
Life is messy. It's complicated.

00:05:35.367 --> 00:05:38.004
We mess up.
Other people mess up.

00:05:38.004 --> 00:05:39.706
We fall in love.
We fall out of love.

00:05:39.706 --> 00:05:45.544
But the truth is, I saw the ways
in which I was colluding with,

00:05:45.544 --> 00:05:47.246
like almost sleeping with
the enemy.

00:05:47.246 --> 00:05:49.415
The way I was agreeing
with them.

00:05:49.415 --> 00:05:51.817
They betrayed me.
I betrayed myself.

00:05:51.817 --> 00:05:53.485
I picked them.
I stayed with them.

00:05:53.485 --> 00:05:55.254
I did all of these things

00:05:55.254 --> 00:05:57.489
because I couldn't do better
at the time.

00:05:57.489 --> 00:06:01.394
And then I went, "What if --
If I put myself there,

00:06:01.394 --> 00:06:03.562
then I can take myself out."

00:06:03.562 --> 00:06:07.533
And that is what this book did
for me, is it helped me

00:06:07.533 --> 00:06:11.170
look at all of my stories that
I've been telling my whole life.

00:06:11.170 --> 00:06:13.472
You know how we have the stories
we're born with,

00:06:13.472 --> 00:06:14.874
our families have told us?

00:06:14.874 --> 00:06:17.510
They're just -- We accept
that they're just facts.

00:06:17.510 --> 00:06:20.579
And then I realized there's a
lot of things that weren't true.

00:06:20.579 --> 00:06:22.781
-That must've been incredibly
cathartic to work through.

00:06:22.781 --> 00:06:25.150
-It was just revelatory because

00:06:25.150 --> 00:06:31.523
I realized that I can change
my story, and so can we all.

00:06:31.523 --> 00:06:34.826
And I just think everyone should
do it just because

00:06:34.826 --> 00:06:37.363
it's an opportunity to give
yourself a better life.

00:06:37.363 --> 00:06:38.730
-Well, that's a really
nice thing

00:06:38.730 --> 00:06:40.166
that you did for yourself,
I think.

00:06:40.166 --> 00:06:42.668
-I feel very happy about it.

00:06:42.668 --> 00:06:45.404
I mean, I don't know, I just
feel like they should teach it

00:06:45.404 --> 00:06:49.708
in school -- how to tell your
truth without blame or shame.

00:06:49.708 --> 00:06:53.211
-I want to close
with one final thing.

00:06:53.211 --> 00:06:54.780
You had a friendship,

00:06:54.780 --> 00:06:57.249
and I think this dates when
the friendship is from,

00:06:57.249 --> 00:06:58.850
because you have Polaroids
of it.

00:06:58.850 --> 00:07:02.088
-Correct, yeah.
-But this is you and Madonna.

00:07:02.088 --> 00:07:03.856
Now, did you reach out to
Madonna before

00:07:03.856 --> 00:07:05.291
you wrote the book and say,
"Hey?"

00:07:05.291 --> 00:07:07.326
-Well, I didn't reach out for
permission or anything, no.

00:07:07.326 --> 00:07:08.760
-Okay, gotcha.
You just gave her a heads-up.

00:07:08.760 --> 00:07:09.995
-That would be silly.

00:07:09.995 --> 00:07:11.797
No, I reached out to her
afterwards.

00:07:11.797 --> 00:07:13.966
I said, "Hey, I wrote about you
in my book,"

00:07:13.966 --> 00:07:17.570
because we were
really, really wild

00:07:17.570 --> 00:07:19.438
and had incredible fun times.

00:07:19.438 --> 00:07:21.974
And I remember, every time
I would hear the song

00:07:21.974 --> 00:07:25.144
"Express Yourself," I would
remember her saying to me,

00:07:25.144 --> 00:07:27.513
"Hey, get in my car. I just
recorded a song. Come here."

00:07:27.513 --> 00:07:28.814
And I said, "Why in your car?"

00:07:28.814 --> 00:07:30.783
"'Cause my sound system is
better. Get in my car."

00:07:30.783 --> 00:07:34.387
And we sat in my driveway, and
she played "Express Yourself,"

00:07:34.387 --> 00:07:38.658
and she said, "It was inspired
by what I want you to know,

00:07:38.658 --> 00:07:40.225
what I think you should do."

00:07:40.225 --> 00:07:41.360
-That's really lovely.

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:43.595
-By my breakup
and by what she believed.

00:07:43.595 --> 00:07:46.665
You know how we believe better
lives for each other?

00:07:46.665 --> 00:07:49.234
-Mm-hmm.
-And I wrote to her

00:07:49.234 --> 00:07:50.470
in the e-mail recently, I said,

00:07:50.470 --> 00:07:52.004
"Did you just say that
to everyone?"

00:07:52.004 --> 00:07:53.472
[ Laughter ]

00:07:53.472 --> 00:07:55.341
So, when she wrote me back,
she didn't say anything,

00:07:55.341 --> 00:07:57.009
so either she didn't read
the whole e-mail...

00:07:57.009 --> 00:07:58.611
[ Laughter ]
She was just like, "Yeah."

00:07:58.611 --> 00:07:59.812
-I think that's confirmation.

00:07:59.812 --> 00:08:01.213
"it's only about you.
It's only about you."

00:08:01.213 --> 00:08:02.514
Hey, thank you so much
for being here.

00:08:02.514 --> 00:08:05.350
Congratulations on the book.
Jennifer Grey, everybody!

00:08:05.350 --> 00:08:07.353
"Out of the Corner"
is available now.

